New humanoid robots keep being announced as the one that will finally do your chores. Some can be reserved, and some exist only as a video and a press release. This guide sorts four of them by what is actually available, as of 12 November 2026, using each maker's own pages first and press reports second.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the sources. Stackbrook did not test any robot, and has no affiliate relationship with any of the companies at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated, and product photos are not used because they are the makers' property.*

## The four status labels

Each robot below gets one label, defined by what a reader can do right now.

- **On sale:** you can pay and the product ships to you on a stated date.
- **Pre-order:** you can pay a deposit or reserve one, but delivery is a promise.
- **Announced:** the company has unveiled the robot, with no price or order page for homes.
- **Rumor:** the company has said nothing; the only source is press reporting based on unnamed sources.

![Status ladder showing the Apple tabletop robot as a rumor, Figure 03 as announced, 1X NEO and Weave Isaac 1 as pre-order, and Apple HomeView, which is not a robot, as the only item with a firm price and ship date](/images/site1/home-robots-2026-on-sale-vs-demo-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the makers' pages and press reports cited in this article.*

## 1X NEO: pre-order

1X's order page lists two options. The maker says Early Access costs $20,000, with a $200 deposit that it calls fully refundable, and the Standard tier is $499 a month. The page's delivery line reads "US Deliveries start 2026." The original announcement, dated 28 October 2025, said first orders would ship to consumer homes in 2026 and that the subscription would ship later.

Whether deliveries have begun is where the sources disagree. On 30 April 2026, 1X's own factory post said it had opened a plant in Hayward, California, and that current units were going first to its internal team for testing in homes. The Next Web reported on 1 May that first customer shipments were still planned for 2026. Aggregator headlines also say NEO is shipping, but their text only repeats plans. On 22 October, Startup Fortune ran a headline saying 1X had started delivering. Its own article, however, says that as of mid-October it had no independent confirmation that any customer outside 1X's early-access program had a NEO at home, and it quotes 1X saying "some of you will get your NEO this year, some will get them later."

We found no such report either. So this guide labels NEO pre-order, not on sale. 1X's own pages say current units are going to its internal team and that customer shipping starts in 2026. Startup Fortune says units are going to early-access customers, but it cites no independent confirmation. If your delivery date matters, ask 1X for it in writing.

## Weave Isaac 1: pre-order

Weave's pages are clearer. The maker says Isaac 1 costs $7,999 up front or $449 a month, with an optional $99 a month premium membership. A $250 deposit reserves one and, the page says, is fully refundable. Weave says "Deliveries to California begin in Fall 2026," with broader US availability in 2027. The Next Web reported in July that deliveries would start in September; Weave's own page says only fall, which runs to late December.

Two inconsistencies are worth knowing. The page's description says "we won't charge you until we ship", yet the page asks for a $250 refundable deposit today, so read the pre-order agreement to see how the two fit. And Weave's home page says "shipping today" for Isaac 0, an earlier and different robot, while Isaac 1 is listed as "First shipments in fall of 2026." Stackbrook did not research Isaac 0.

As for what it does, the maker says Isaac 1 finds and picks up dirty clothes, folds and puts away clean ones, makes beds and returns toys and shoes to their places. Depending on the home, it adds, it may do more, such as loading washers and dryers. The Next Web, summarising the launch, said the robot does not operate the washer.

## Figure 03: announced

Figure unveiled Figure 03 on 9 October 2025 and says it designed it with homes in mind. On 29 April 2026 the company said it had delivered over 350 Figure 03 robots from its BotQ factory. It said those robots go to internal research, data collection, household-task development and commercial use-case development. That page does not say robots are sold to consumers and gives no price, order process or delivery date.

On 17 September 2026 Figure published results from 30 unseen Bay Area homes. The maker says its index-pretrained policy succeeded on 56 percent of zero-shot trials across three tasks (living-room tidying, towel folding and bed making), counting full completion only. That is a research result, not a service you can hire, so Figure 03 is labelled announced.

## Apple's tabletop robot: rumor, and a real product next to it

Apple has announced no robot. The reporting began with Bloomberg's Mark Gurman, as relayed by Engadget on 13 August 2025: a tabletop device, essentially a display on a motorized arm, targeted for 2027. On 18 October 2026, 9to5Mac relayed Gurman's newsletter saying a roughly 9-inch HomeView variant with a motorized arm, code-named J595, is planned for 2027 or 2028. On 2 November, The Information reported, via 9to5Mac and The Next Web, that Apple hopes to launch it in 2027 but it could slip to 2028 because of software, and The Next Web put the price near $1,000. These reports rest on unnamed sources, and Apple has confirmed nothing. MacRumors' guide, whose metadata shows October 2025, describes the robot as "reportedly" planned.

Do not confuse this with Apple HomeView, which is real. Apple's newsroom post of 13 October 2026 says pre-orders started that day, with availability beginning 13 November, at $299 in the US, so it is on pre-order today. Apple also says it is not sold in the European Union. It is a smart display, and Apple's release does not mention a robot.

## What a robot with remote operators means for privacy

Both pre-order robots lean on people. 1X says that for complex tasks NEO does not know, an Expert from 1X "can remotely supervise its actions at scheduled times," and its page also describes piloting NEO from anywhere via the app and a VR device. Weave says Isaac 1 is autonomous for its two main routines by default, with "teleoperation assistance when needed to guarantee we complete tasks." Weave's page does not say who the operators are or how often they step in.

On privacy specifically, the sources are thin and mostly company claims. Startup Fortune reports that 1X says it blurs faces, lets owners set no-go zones and controls when remote access is possible, and that operators watch a live camera feed, the last being the article's own description of how remote operation works. It attributes the 1X claims to the company and does not verify them. Road to VR, in a report that is undated on the page but appears to date from 2025, covered a Wall Street Journal demo in which the outlet says tasks were teleoperated, and quoted 1X's chief executive saying "If we don't have your data, we can't make the product better." Weave's Isaac 1 page says "Privacy is core to Isaac 1's design" and that the robot works only when you want it to. Neither maker's order page answered, in the text we could read, who the operators are, how long footage is kept or whether you can say no to data sharing. Ask for those answers before paying.

## What to do with this

If you want a robot that folds laundry in your own home this year, the honest answer is that the only realistic candidates are pre-orders with refundable deposits and delivery dates still unconfirmed by any independent buyer. Weave's California-first rollout is the most specific. If you are outside California, the makers' own pages point to 2027 or later.

Before reserving anything, check that the deposit really is refundable on the checkout page, ask for a written delivery date, and ask what remote operators can see.
